<Article
  title="Thirst Contact: water from the sky, bottled in your backyard"
  description="Return to Aura. One beautiful room at a time."
  kicker="Water"
  date="2026-09-30"
>
  <p>An atmospheric water generator pulls moisture out of the air and turns it into drinking water. On Thirst Contact, it is a low unit on the roof. Inside, a short line fills the bottles.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">What it's for</RevealHeading>
  <p>If you have some humidity and power, you can make drinking water without a well. Thirst Contact is an Aura room with a standard atmospheric water generator built in: water from the sky, bottled in your backyard. Like every Aura room it is at the concept stage, so the waitlist is the way in. How much water you get depends on humidity, temperature and power, so we do not quote numbers. Dry winter air makes less.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">The bottles</RevealHeading>
  <p>The line fills plastic jugs because they are light, familiar and easy to label. Bottling for yourself? Leave them plain. The bigger plan, where bottled water feeds a free water network, is in <a href="/journal/free-food-and-water">the Aura Network note</a>. Water that leaves your property still has to follow local rules. The machine does not change that.</p>
  <RevealHeading as="h2">Still a nice room</RevealHeading>
  <p>The bottling line takes up a corner, not the whole room. Polycarbonate, oak and the view all stay. See the setup on <a href="/water">the Thirst Contact page</a>, and the room it is built on, <a href="/#shell">on your land</a>.</p>
</Article>
